   "title": "Module 2: Radix Conversions & Power-of-2 Shortcut Methods",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.1 Any Base $r$ to Decimal (Base 10) via Polynomial Expansion"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "To evaluate any number $(N)_r$ in decimal, evaluate its positional power series in base 10:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "(N)_r = \\sum_{i=-m}^{n-1} d_i \\cdot r^i"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Worked Example 2.1.1 (Slide 242):** Convert $(110101.101)_2$ to Decimal."
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "(110101.101)_2 = 1\\cdot 2^5 + 1\\cdot 2^4 + 0\\cdot 2^3 + 1\\cdot 2^2 + 0\\cdot 2^1 + 1\\cdot 2^0 + 1\\cdot 2^{-1} + 0\\cdot 2^{-2} + 1\\cdot 2^{-3}"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "= 32 + 16 + 0 + 4 + 0 + 1 + 0.5 + 0 + 0.125 = (53.625)_{10}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Worked Example 2.1.2 (Slide 243):** Convert $(347.25)_8$ to Decimal."
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "(347.25)_8 = 3\\cdot 8^2 + 4\\cdot 8^1 + 7\\cdot 8^0 + 2\\cdot 8^{-1} + 5\\cdot 8^{-2}"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "= 3\\cdot 64 + 4\\cdot 8 + 7\\cdot 1 + \\frac{2}{8} + \\frac{5}{64} = 192 + 32 + 7 + 0.25 + 0.078125 = (231.328125)_{10}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Worked Example 2.1.3 (Slide 244):** Convert $(\\text{2B}3.\\text{C})_{16}$ to Decimal."
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "(\\text{2B}3.\\text{C})_{16} = 2\\cdot 16^2 + 11\\cdot 16^1 + 3\\cdot 16^0 + 12\\cdot 16^{-1}"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "= 512 + 176 + 3 + 0.75 = (691.75)_{10}"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.2 Decimal (Base 10) to Any Base $r$"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "The conversion separates into two distinct, non-interacting algorithms:"
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "items": [
      "**Integer Part $\\to$ Successive Division Method:**"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "Repeatedly divide the integer quotient by base $r$.",
      "Record remainders until the quotient is $0$.",
      "Collect remainders from **bottom to top (MSD to LSD)**."
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "items": [
      "**Fractional Part $\\to$ Successive Multiplication Method:**"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "Repeatedly multiply the fractional portion by base $r$.",
      "Record the extracted integer part at each step.",
      "Continue multiplying the remaining fraction until it reaches $0$ or the desired precision.",
      "Collect extracted integers from **top to bottom (MSD to LSD)**."
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "alert",
     "type": "NOTE",
     "text": "**Slide 248 Instructor Division Slip & Verification:**\nIn Slide 248, the classroom chalkboard illustrates converting $737_{10}$ to octal:\n- $737 \\div 8 = 92$, Remainder $= 1$ (LSD)\n- $92 \\div 8 = 11$, Remainder $= 4$\n- $11 \\div 8 = 1$, Remainder $= 3$\n- $1 \\div 8 = 0$, Remainder $= 1$ (MSD)\nReading remainders bottom-up yields $(1341)_8$.  \n*Faculty Note:* A minor handwriting transcription slip originally read $739 \\div 8$, but the remainder sequence $(1, 4, 3, 1)$ belongs uniquely to $737_{10} = (1341)_8$."
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Worked Example 2.2.1 (Fractional Decimal to Binary):** Convert $(0.6875)_{10}$ to Binary."
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "$0.6875 \\times 2 = 1.375 \\implies$ integer $1$ (MSD), remaining fraction $0.375$",
      "$0.375 \\times 2 = 0.75 \\implies$ integer $0$, remaining fraction $0.75$",
      "$0.75 \\times 2 = 1.50 \\implies$ integer $1$, remaining fraction $0.50$",
      "$0.50 \\times 2 = 1.00 \\implies$ integer $1$ (LSD), remaining fraction $0.00$ (terminates)"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Reading top-down: $(0.6875)_{10} = (0.1011)_2$."
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.3 Arbitrary Base $A$ to Arbitrary Base $B$ Conversion via Decimal Bridge (Slide 251)"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "When bases $A$ and $B$ are not integer powers of each other ($A \\neq B^k$), the error-free systematic route is:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\text{Base } A \\xrightarrow{\\text{Polynomial Expansion}} \\text{Decimal (Base 10)} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Successive Division/Multiplication}} \\text{Base } B"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Worked Example 2.3.1 (Slide 251):** Convert $(341.12)_5$ to Base 6."
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "items": [
      "*Base 5 to Decimal:*"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "(341.12)_5 = 3\\cdot 5^2 + 4\\cdot 5^1 + 1\\cdot 5^0 + 1\\cdot 5^{-1} + 2\\cdot 5^{-2} = 75 + 20 + 1 + 0.2 + 0.08 = (96.28)_{10}"
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "items": [
      "*Decimal to Base 6:*"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "Integer: $96 \\div 6 = 16$ (rem $0$); $16 \\div 6 = 2$ (rem $4$); $2 \\div 6 = 0$ (rem $2$) $\\implies (240)_6$.",
      "Fraction: $0.28 \\times 6 = 1.68$ (int $1$); $0.68 \\times 6 = 4.08$ (int $4$); $0.08 \\times 6 = 0.48$ (int $0$) $\\implies (0.140)_6$.",
      "Result: $(341.12)_5 = (240.140)_6$."
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.4 Power-of-2 Shortcut Highways ($2 \\leftrightarrow 2^k$) (Slides 252\u2013255)"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Because $8 = 2^3, 16 = 2^4, 32 = 2^5$, each octal digit corresponds to exactly $3$ bits, each hex digit to $4$ bits, and each base-32 digit to $5$ bits."
    },
    {
     "t": "alert",
     "type": "CAUTION",
     "text": "**The Radix-Point Centering Rule (Never Violate):**\nWhen grouping bits into clusters of $k$:\n- **Integer Part:** Group from **Right to Left** (radix point toward MSD $\\leftarrow$). Pad leading zeros to the leftmost group if incomplete.\n- **Fractional Part:** Group from **Left to Right** (radix point toward LSD $\\rightarrow$). Pad trailing zeros to the rightmost group if incomplete.\n*Fatal Trap:* Padding zeros on the wrong end alters the numerical magnitude!"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Worked Example 2.4.1 (Slide 255):** Convert $(734.26)_8$ to Base 4 ($4 = 2^2$)."
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "items": [
      "Expand each octal digit into 3 binary bits:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "7 \\to 111, \\quad 3 \\to 011, \\quad 4 \\to 100, \\quad . \\quad 2 \\to 010, \\quad 6 \\to 110"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "(734.26)_8 = (111\\,011\\,100\\,.\\,010\\,110)_2"
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "items": [
      "Re-group into 2-bit clusters for Base 4:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "Integer: $(01\\,11\\,01\\,11\\,00)_2 \\to (1\\,3\\,1\\,3\\,0)_4$ (padded leading $0$).",
      "Fraction: $(01\\,01\\,10)_2 \\to (1\\,1\\,2)_4$.",
      "Result: $(734.26)_8 = (13130.112)_4$."
     ]
    }
   ]
